---

An Oriel window can be drawn three ways: by the platform's web view, by
Oriel's [native renderer]($link.page('docs/native-renderer')) running the
same HTML, CSS and JavaScript without a browser, or by the experimental
Oriel renderer, which runs views written in `.oriel` markup from Zig with no
JavaScript at all. They are front ends over one stack: each renderer turns
its own language into native views, and everything under that (the engine
machinery, the input rules, layout, drawing, the platform's widgets) and
everything beside it (commands, modules, permissions, the platform layer)
is written once and shared.

This page is the map: which layer owns what, what each renderer adds, and
where new behavior goes. [Concepts]($link.page('docs/concepts')) covers the
app's side (the process, the bridge, commands and events).

# [The layers]($heading.id('layers'))

>[]($block.attrs("mermaid"))
```
flowchart TB
  subgraph App["The app (Zig)"]
    Cmd["Commands, events,<br/>app state"]
    Mods["Modules: tray, files, sql,<br/>local AI, camera, ..."]
  end
  subgraph Front["Front ends: one per renderer"]
    WV["WebView<br/>the platform's browser engine"]
    NW["Native renderer<br/>QuickJS, native DOM, CSS,<br/>main.js, render.js"]
    OR["Oriel renderer (experimental)<br/>.oriel views, ui.zig, focus.zig"]
  end
  subgraph Shared["Shared native UI (src/native_ui)"]
    Core["Engine core<br/>engine_core.zig"]
    Rules["Input rules<br/>interaction.zig"]
    Tree["Node tree and layout<br/>tree.zig, Yoga, text"]
    BE["Backends<br/>gtk, win32, appkit, uikit, android"]
  end
  Plat["Platform layer<br/>src/platform: windows, event loop, bridge"]
  Cmd <-->|"invoke, events<br/>(window.oriel)"| WV
  Cmd <-->|"invoke, events<br/>(window.oriel)"| NW
  Cmd <-->|"ui.invoke, Driver<br/>(direct Zig calls)"| OR
  NW --> Core
  OR --> Core
  NW -->|"host.tabNext, focusRing, ..."| Rules
  OR --> Rules
  Core --> Tree --> BE
  Mods --> Plat
  WV --> Plat
  BE --> Plat
```

Each layer only calls the layers under it. A front end never draws and
never reads a platform API; a backend never knows which front end built the
nodes it draws. That is what lets a fix or a feature in a lower layer reach
every renderer at once.

| Layer | Source | Owns |
|---|---|---|
| App | the app's Zig | Commands, events, state, which modules it turns on |
| Modules | `src/modules/*` | Tray, notifications, files, SQL, local AI, ...: the same API under every renderer ([Modules]($link.page('docs/modules'))) |
| Front end | WebView: the platform's; native: `src/native_ui/js/src`, `engine_js.zig`, `dom/`; Oriel: `oriel_markup.zig`, `ui.zig`, `engine_zig.zig`, `focus.zig` | Its language: parsing it, styling, deciding which native nodes exist and what they say, delivering events to the app's code |
| Engine core | `src/native_ui/engine_core.zig` | The open engines, frame callbacks, painting at most once a frame, scrolling, the key scroll's glide |
| Input rules | `src/native_ui/interaction.zig` | How the platform's WebView answers the keyboard and pointer: focus rings by kind of element, Tab's order, what a click focuses, which keys activate, how far a key scrolls |
| Node tree | `src/native_ui/tree.zig`, `text_measure_cache.zig`, `svg_path.zig`, `zig_canvas.zig` | One node per drawn view: Yoga layout, paint order, scrolling, hit testing, accessibility entries, tooltips |
| Backends | `src/native_ui/gtk.zig`, `win32.zig`, `appkit.zig`, `uikit.zig` with `apple_draw.zig`, `android.zig` with `OrielNative.kt` | Drawing, text measurement, the platform's widgets (fields, buttons, checkboxes), input events, the screen reader |
| Platform layer | `src/platform/<os>` | Windows, the event loop, the web view and its bridge, the command policy ([Concepts]($link.page('docs/concepts').ref('platform-layer'))) |

# [The three renderers]($heading.id('renderers'))

| | WebView | Native renderer | Oriel renderer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Build | default | `-Dnative_ui` | `-Dzig_ui` (experimental) |
| You write | HTML, CSS, JavaScript | the same files, unchanged | `.oriel` views and Zig |
| Runs the UI | the platform's browser engine, in its own processes | QuickJS-ng and Oriel's native DOM, in the app's process | compiled views and the app's Zig, in the app's process |
| Draws with | the browser engine | the shared tree and backends | the shared tree and backends |
| Talks to Zig | `window.oriel`: messages to the app | `window.oriel`: direct calls in the process | Zig calls: `ui.invoke`, the `Driver` callbacks |
| Status | stable, every platform | experimental: Linux, Windows, macOS, Android, iOS | experimental, not yet for apps: Linux; builds for Android |

The web view shares the app, the modules and the platform layer; the two
native renderers also share the whole native UI stack. In the native
renderer the front end is JavaScript because the page is; in the Oriel
renderer there is none, so a view's update is a few Zig calls instead of a
DOM mutation, a restyle and a flatten.

Measured on the [system monitor]($link.page('docs/examples')) (2026-10-13,
Linux, three runs each, software rendering on a private X display, the same
2 s sampling): the Oriel renderer opens its window in 0.18 s against 0.25 s,
uses 0.70% CPU idle against 0.97% and 5.8% while scrolling the process list
against 11.3%. Memory is about the same (246 MB RSS each): most of it is the
toolkit and graphics stack both load, and leaving out QuickJS and the DOM
saves 6 to 8 MB of heap.

# [What is shared, and how]($heading.id('shared'))

**One tree, any front end.** Both native front ends build the same
`tree.zig` nodes with the same props: the native renderer from the styled
DOM (`render.js`), the Oriel renderer from compiled `.oriel` templates
(`ui.zig`). Layout, text, scrolling, hit testing and drawing don't know
which. A prop added to the tree and drawn by a backend is available to both:
tooltips (`tip`) are one prop and one backend change.

**One engine core.** Each window has one engine, `engine_js.zig` or
`engine_zig.zig`, and both are thin: their front end's lifecycle and
events. The rest is `engine_core.zig`: the engines by serial (a Zig handle
that outlived its window finds nothing), the app's display frame callbacks
(a callback may cancel itself or add another during a frame), changes
painted at most once per display frame however many events came, scroll
offsets, and the glide that animates a key scroll on display frames.

**One set of input rules.** How a window answers the keyboard is measured
from each platform's WebView and kept in `interaction.zig`: the focus ring
WebKitGTK, WebView2, Android's WebView and WKWebView draw around each kind
of element; which elements Tab visits on macOS without Full Keyboard Access
and on iOS; a radio group being one Tab stop; WebKit not focusing a button
on a click; Enter and Space activating; the 40 px and 87.5% a key scrolls
and the curves it glides on. The Oriel renderer applies them to its tree.
The native renderer's page keeps what only a page can do (dispatching
`keydown`, honouring `preventDefault`, finding candidates in document order)
and asks the engine for the decisions (`host.tabNext`, `host.clickFocuses`,
`host.activates`, `host.keyScroll`, `host.focusRing`). The runtime's Node
tests get the same rules compiled to WebAssembly
(`src/native_ui/interaction_wasm.zig`, `zig build interaction-wasm`).

**One accessibility tree.** Both front ends describe their elements as
entries in `Tree.ax` (role, name, states): the native renderer from the
DOM's roles and ARIA (`a11y.js`), the Oriel renderer from its elements and
`role`/`label` attributes (`Tree.setAxEntry`). The backends hand them to the
screen reader.

**Modules don't know the renderer.** A module is Zig on the app's side of
the bridge. Under the web view and the native renderer the page reaches it
through the same `window.oriel` (`src/core/bridge_api.js`), checked by the
same command policy (`src/core/native_commands.zig`); under the Oriel
renderer the app's Zig calls it directly. Turning a module on is the same
build flag in all three ([Modules]($link.page('docs/modules'))).

# [Where new behavior goes]($heading.id('where'))

Put a behavior in the lowest layer that can own it, so every renderer gets
it:

| It is about | It goes in | Example |
|---|---|---|
| How something is drawn or what widget shows it | the backends, through a tree prop | a tooltip, a native checkbox |
| Where things are, what's under the pointer, scrolling | `tree.zig` | sticky positioning, hit testing |
| How the platform's browser answers input | `interaction.zig` | Tab's order on macOS, the focus ring's offset |
| When things run and get painted | `engine_core.zig` | frame callbacks, one paint per frame |
| A language: HTML/CSS/DOM, or `.oriel` | that front end | CSS's cascade, an `.oriel` element |
| Something the app or the page calls | a module or a command | the tray, SQL |

A rule that both front ends need is written once, in Zig, and the front end
that can't call Zig directly (the page's JavaScript) asks through a host
function. A front end keeps only what belongs to its language.

# [Background]($heading.id('background'))

Oriel started from two apps' needs. [GhostPen](https://ghostpen.foreachdeveloper.com/),
AI text editing anywhere on the desktop, needed a tray, global shortcuts,
typing into other apps, the clipboard, a single instance and network calls.
GhostReel, local video search and AI-assisted editing, needed media
streaming, dialogs, SQLite with vector search, whisper.cpp, llama.cpp and
packaging. Both started in Rust; its build sizes and iteration time were
the reason to start over in Zig 0.16, which gives small binaries, direct C
interop, `comptime` reflection and one build system for desktop and mobile
targets.

# [Limits]($heading.id('limits'))

- The native renderer doesn't promise full browser compatibility. CSS, DOM,
  accessibility and JavaScript behavior it doesn't support yet belongs on the
  web view until it's implemented and tested.
- Native renderers keep the UI thread's affinity: commands and model work
  run on workers, and their results go back to the platform's main loop.
- Linux spans GTK, Wayland, X11 and several portal implementations; macOS,
  Windows and the phones each need their own SDK and testing on the real
  platform.
- Zig 0.16 and several packages around it still move: dependency versions
  stay pinned and tested in CI.
- Security is part of the framework's contract: capabilities, asset origins,
  navigation and IPC validation change together with each new module.

